Based on the repro (a graticule that mysteriously “drops” most meridians and cuts off parallels whenever the view straddles the antimeridian), the culprit is in `createGraticule_()`’s computation of the valid lon‐lat extent:

```js
let validExtent = [
  Math.max(extent[0], this.minLonP_),
  Math.max(extent[1], this.minLatP_),
  Math.min(extent[2], this.maxLonP_),
  Math.min(extent[3], this.maxLatP_)
];
validExtent = transformExtent(validExtent, this.projection_, 'EPSG:4326');
const maxLat = validExtent[3];
const maxLon = validExtent[2];
const minLat = validExtent[1];
const minLon = validExtent[0];
```

When the view crosses the dateline, after `transformExtent()` the “minLon” comes out numerically larger than “maxLon” (e.g. [170, …, –170]), which effectively kills both the meridian‐loop and the parallel‐loop. The simplest remedy is: as soon as you detect `validExtent[0] > validExtent[2]`, treat the longitude span as the full world (i.e. from this.minLon_ to this.maxLon_). Insert that test right after the `transformExtent()` call:

